WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:01.900
- [Instructor] A cesarean section,

00:00:01.900 --> 00:00:05.590
often referred to as a C-section,
is a surgical procedure

00:00:05.590 --> 00:00:07.070
in which incisions are made

00:00:07.070 --> 00:00:10.820
in a woman's abdomen and
uterus to deliver a baby.

00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:13.210
Some cesarean sections are planned.

00:00:13.210 --> 00:00:14.640
More often, however,

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:17.130
the need for the
procedure becomes apparent

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:18.860
after the onset of labor,

00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:22.410
when abnormal conditions make
a vaginal delivery unsafe

00:00:22.410 --> 00:00:24.870
for the mother or her baby.

00:00:24.870 --> 00:00:28.470
Common indications for
cesarean section include

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:33.300
dystocia, placenta previa
and fetal distress.

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:37.170
Dystocia or prolonged,
non-progressive labor can occur

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:39.270
when the baby's head is unable to fit

00:00:39.270 --> 00:00:40.460
through the birth canal

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:43.380
or its body is in an unfavorable position,

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:47.160
such as perpendicular to the
birth canal or buttocks first,

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:49.720
which is the breech position.

00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:53.950
Placenta previa occurs when a
low-lying placenta partially

00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:57.030
or completely blocks the cervical opening.

00:00:57.030 --> 00:00:58.740
Fetal distress occurs

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:02.070
whenever the health of the
baby is in imminent danger,

00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:04.220
usually from inadequate blood flow

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:06.950
through the placenta or umbilical cord.

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:09.900
Fetal distress can occur
when the placenta separates

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:12.600
from the wall of the
uterus prior to delivery

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:16.820
or the umbilical cord becomes
compressed or squeezed.

00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:19.960
Other conditions that may
require a cesarean section

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:24.580
include multiple births,
large tumors of the uterus,

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:28.550
genital herpes or other
infections, or medical problems

00:01:28.550 --> 00:01:32.280
such as uncontrolled
diabetes or hypertension.

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:34.750
Your doctor may use ultrasound testing

00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:37.450
and a fetal heart monitor to help decide

00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:40.710
whether your baby should
be delivered by cesarean.

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:43.530
When a cesarean section becomes necessary,

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:45.770
you will be prepped for surgery.

00:01:45.770 --> 00:01:47.610
If not already in place,

00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:50.090
an intravenous line will be started,

00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:51.950
and a catheter will be inserted

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:54.700
into your bladder to drain urine.

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:57.830
In the operating room, you
will be given anesthesia.

00:01:57.830 --> 00:02:01.330
In most cases, a spinal
anesthetic is administered

00:02:01.330 --> 00:02:03.860
to numb the lower portion of your body.

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:07.770
Sometimes, however, a general
anesthetic will be used.

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:09.030
Your doctor will begin

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:11.670
by making an incision in your abdomen.

00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:13.880
It will either be a vertical incision

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:17.600
from just below the navel to
the top of the pubic bone,

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:20.650
or more frequently, a horizontal incision

00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:23.610
across and just above the pubic bone.

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:25.960
This is often called a bikini cut.

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:28.550
Your doctor will then
make a second incision

00:02:28.550 --> 00:02:30.810
on the lower part of the uterus.

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:32.530
Once the uterus has opened,

00:02:32.530 --> 00:02:35.100
your doctor will rupture the amniotic sac

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:38.270
if it is still intact
and deliver the baby.

00:02:38.270 --> 00:02:40.740
The time from the initial
abdominal incision

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:43.483
to birth is typically five minutes.

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:49.393
Your doctor will then clamp
and cut the umbilical cord,

00:02:52.070 --> 00:02:54.090
gently remove the placenta,

00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:57.000
and tightly suture your
uterus and abdomen.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:00.400
This typically takes about 45 minutes.

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:03.140
The hospital stay after a cesarean section

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:05.810
is usually three to five days.

00:03:05.810 --> 00:03:07.960
During this time you will be encouraged

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:10.850
to breastfeed, nap when the baby sleeps,

00:03:10.850 --> 00:03:12.800
and get out of bed often.

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:14.200
While most patients are able

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:17.410
to take care of their new
baby soon after the procedure,

00:03:17.410 --> 00:03:20.450
full recovery may take six to eight weeks.

00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:23.223
Your scar will lighten as it heals.

00:03:25.936 --> 00:03:29.519
(upbeat synthesizer music)

